## Additional Unity Guidelines (Recommended Additions)

### Coding Conventions

- Always specify access modifiers (`private` by default). Avoid implicit private fields.
- Use `readonly` for fields that are set only in constructors/Awake and never mutate.
- Prefer expression-bodied properties for trivial getters; avoid public fields except `const` or `static readonly`.
- Use `nameof(Symbol)` for event names, Animator parameters, and logs to prevent string drift.
- Prefer `CompareTag("Tag")` over `gameObject.tag == "Tag"`.

### Unity Lifecycle Best Practices

- Cache component references in `Awake`/`OnEnable`; avoid calling `GetComponent` in `Update`.
- Use `FixedUpdate` only for physics; scale motion by `Time.fixedDeltaTime`. In `Update`, use `Time.deltaTime`.
- Unsubscribe events and release resources in `OnDisable`/`OnDestroy`.
- Avoid heavy work in `Update`; consider events, coroutines, or `InvokeRepeating` with sensible intervals.

### Memory & GC

- Avoid per-frame allocations (e.g., LINQ in hot paths, string concatenation in `Update`).
- Reuse lists/arrays with preallocated capacity; clear instead of reallocate.
- Use object pooling for bullets, VFX, and transient objects (instantiate once, enable/disable).
- Avoid `new WaitForSeconds(...)` each frame; cache common yield instructions when appropriate.

### Addressables, Assets & Scenes

- Prefer Addressables for runtime content loading/unloading; release handles explicitly.
- Keep one active scene for gameplay plus additive scenes for UI/lighting when suitable.
- Validate asset references at build time; fail early with clear errors if missing.
- Never modify imported assets at runtime; instantiate runtime copies when needed.

### Physics & Layers

- Use `LayerMask` and Physics settings to limit collisions; avoid broad queries.
- Prefer `Rigidbody.MovePosition/MoveRotation` in `FixedUpdate` for kinematic motion.
- Use triggers vs. collision thoughtfully; set `Physics.queriesHitTriggers` intentionally.

### UI & Input

- Use TextMeshPro exclusively; avoid legacy `Text`.
- Prefer Unity Input System (new) with action maps; avoid polling in `Update` when events suffice.
- Batch UI updates to minimize layout rebuilds and canvas re-renders.

### Logging & Diagnostics

- Use `Debug.Log`, `LogWarning`, `LogError` judiciously; gate noisy logs behind `#if UNITY_EDITOR` or a verbose flag.
- Include categories via prefixes or a simple logger wrapper (e.g., `[AI]`, `[Net]`).
- Use the Profiler, Deep Profile, and Memory Profiler when optimizing; verify wins with measurements.

### Editor Tooling

- Annotate inspector fields with `[Header]`, `[Tooltip]`, and `[Min/Range]` to improve usability.
- Provide context menus and custom inspectors for complex components.
- Wrap editor-only utilities with `#if UNITY_EDITOR` to avoid runtime bloat.

### Testing & Validation

- Write PlayMode and EditMode tests for core systems; decouple pure logic to test without Unity runtime.
- Add runtime asserts/guards for invalid state; fail fast with actionable messages.

### Documentation & Comments

- Document public APIs with XML docs; explain intent and constraints, not obvious mechanics.
- Prefer short, high-signal comments above non-trivial code blocks; keep code self-explanatory.
- All in-code comments (XML docs, summaries, remarks, TODOs) must be written in English.

### Response Expectations (for this assistant)

- Default to Spanish responses for explanations; keep code comments in English unless asked.
- When choices exist, propose 2–3 options with pros/cons and a recommended default.
- Provide copy-pastable code that compiles in Unity 2021+ unless specified otherwise.

